    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the codebase's existing conventions.\n\n- **Right unit / consistent with siblings.** `bulkSignupWarning` is a value derived once at creation and persisted, exactly like the adjacent `namesakeCount`, `customerCode`, and `membershipNumber`. Adding it as an `Owner` field with a column, getter/setter, and schema entries mirrors `namesakeCount` line-for-line, so a maintainer would find nothing surprising. Persisting it (rather than computing on read) is also what makes \"returned on read\" work, matching the OpenAPI description.\n- **Assignment is in the right place.** The `owner.setBulkSignupWarning(...)` call sits with the other derived-field assignments in `addOwner`, and reuses the existing `countOwnersRegisteredOn` helper rather than introducing a parallel count path.\n- **Constant is well-placed and documented.** `BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD` lives right next to its close sibling `DAILY_CREATE_LIMIT`, with a Javadoc comment in the same style, and the \"more than 80\" semantics are correctly expressed with `>`.\n- **Cross-cutting edits are complete and uniform.** All four dialect schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) get the column in the same position, and the OpenAPI schema marks it `readOnly` consistent with the other server-assigned fields.\n\nOne very minor, non-blocking observation: `countOwnersRegisteredOn(effectiveRegistrationDate)` is now evaluated twice in `addOwner` (line 123 for the limit check, line 145 for the warning), yielding the same value both times. It could be hoisted to a local, but the surrounding code deliberately favors one self-contained expression per assignment line, so leaving it inline is consistent with the established style and not worth changing.",
